
README
C++ Credit Card Validation Program
A C++ program that validates credit card numbers using the Luhn algorithm.
1. Project Overview
This project is a C++ console application that checks whether a credit card number is valid. The program validates the number based on its length, checks whether it has an accepted prefix, and then applies the Luhn checksum algorithm.
2. Objectives
•	Read a credit card number from the user.
•	Determine the number of digits in the card number.
•	Check whether the card number contains between 13 and 16 digits.
•	Check whether the card begins with an accepted prefix: 4, 5, 37, or 6.
•	Apply the Luhn algorithm to calculate the checksum.
•	Display whether the credit card number is valid or invalid.
3. Technologies Used
•	Programming language: C++
•	Input/output: <iostream>
•	Mathematical functions: <cmath>
•	String library included in the source code: <string>
•	Compiler: Any standard C++ compiler such as g++, MinGW, or Visual Studio C++ compiler
•	Execution environment: Console/terminal
4. Program Structure
The program is divided into the following functions:
•	main(): Controls the program, receives input, calls the validation function, and displays the result.
•	isValid(): Performs the overall validation process.
•	sumOfDoubleEvenPlace(): Doubles the required digits and calculates their contribution to the checksum.
•	getDigit(): Adds the digits of a doubled value when the result is greater than 9.
•	sumOfOddPlace(): Adds the remaining digits without doubling them.
•	prefixMatched(): Checks whether the card number begins with a specified prefix.
•	getSize(): Counts the number of digits in a number.
•	getPrefix(): Extracts the first k digits of a number.
5. How the Program Works
1. The user enters a credit card number.
2. The program calls isValid() to begin validation.
3. getSize() determines how many digits the number contains.
4. The program rejects numbers with fewer than 13 or more than 16 digits.
5. prefixMatched() checks whether the number begins with 4, 5, 37, or 6.
6. sumOfDoubleEvenPlace() processes the required alternating digits by doubling them.
7. getDigit() reduces two-digit doubled values by adding their individual digits.
8. sumOfOddPlace() adds the other digits directly.
9. The two sums are added to produce totalSum.
10. If totalSum is divisible by 10, the program reports that the card is valid; otherwise, it reports that it is invalid.
6. Luhn Algorithm
The program uses the Luhn algorithm, a checksum method commonly used to detect mistyped identification numbers such as payment-card numbers. The algorithm does not prove that a card exists or that it can be used for a transaction; it only checks whether the number satisfies the checksum and the additional rules implemented by this program.
7. How to Compile and Run
Using g++ from a terminal:
g++ credit_card.cpp -o credit_card
.\credit_card.exe
On Linux/macOS, the executable can be run with:
g++ credit_card.cpp -o credit_card
./credit_card
